var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"In the following, you find the documentation of all exported functions of the BartIO.jl package:\n\n","title":"API"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BartIO.bart-Tuple{Int64, Any, Vararg{Array{ComplexF32}}}","page":"API","text":"bart = wrapper_bart(pathtobart::String)\nArgs are concatenated at the end of the command.\n\n### output\n- bart : a wrapper to call bart from Julia through the python functions from the bart repository.\n\nTO DO :\n- add kwargs support like in the python wrapper https://github.com/mrirecon/bart/pull/295\n\nExample :\n````\nbart = wrapper_bart(pathtobart)\nbart(0,\"version\")\n\nbart(0,\"phantom -h\")\nbart(1,\"phantom -k -x128\")\n````\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BartIO.bart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BartIO.read_cfl-Tuple{String}","page":"API","text":"read_cfl(filename::String)\n\nread_cfl(filename(no extension)) -> Array{ComplexF32,N} where N is defined the filename.hdr file\nread_cfl(filename.cfl) -> Array{ComplexF32,N} where N is defined the filename.hdr file\nread_cfl(filename.hdr) -> Array{ComplexF32,N} where N is defined the filename.hdr file\n\nReads complex data from files created by the Berkeley Advanced Reconstruction Toolbox (BART). The output is an Array of ComplexF32 with the dimensions stored in a .hdr file.\n\nParameters:\n\nfilename:   path and filename of the cfl and hdr files, which can either be without extension, end on .cfl, or end on .hdr\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BartIO.read_cfl"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BartIO.set_bart_path-Tuple{String}","page":"API","text":"set_bart_path(pathToBart::String)\n\nOverride the bundled executable with a BART executable at pathToBart. The override is stored in ENV[\"TOOLBOX_PATH\"]; use \"\" to restore the selected bundled executable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BartIO.set_bart_path"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BartIO.write_cfl-Tuple{String, Array{ComplexF32}}","page":"API","text":"write_cfl(filename::String,dataCfl::Array{ComplexF32})\n\nwrite_cfl(filename(no extension),Array{ComplexF32})\nwrite_cfl(filename.cfl, Array{ComplexF32})\nwrite_cfl(filename.hdr,Array{ComplexF32})\n\nWrite complex data to files following the convention of the Berkeley Advanced Reconstruction Toolbox (BART). The input is an Array of ComplexF32 with the dimensions stored in a .hdr file.\n\nParameters:\n\nfilename:   path and filename of the cfl and hdr files, which can either be without extension, end on .cfl, or end on .hdr\nArray{ComplexF32,N}:   Array of ComplexF32 corresponding to image/k-space\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BartIO.write_cfl"},{"category":"section","location":"#BartIO.jl-documentation","page":"Home","text":"BartIO.jl is a Julia package in order to interact with the Berkeley Advanced Reconstruction Toolbox (BART).\n\nThis package offers the possibility to\n\nread and write cfl/hdr files used by BART\nCall BART commands using the CPU executable provided by BART_jll","title":"BartIO.jl documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Read-and-write-files","page":"Home","text":"To load BART data (stored in a .cfl and a .hdr header file), simply call read_cfl(filename), where filename can be either be without a filename extension, or it can include .cfl or .hdr.\n\nTo write BART compatible files, call  write_cfl(filename, x), where filename can be either be without a filename extension, or it can include .cfl or .hdr and x is the data.","title":"Read & write files"},{"category":"section","location":"#Calling-BART-functions","page":"Home","text":"BartIO replicates the functionality of the Python wrapper","title":"Calling BART functions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Requirements","page":"Home","text":"Julia 1.10+. BART_jll bundles CPU-only BART 1 on macOS and Linux (glibc). For bundled BART on Windows, use Julia in WSL2; musl and RISC-V are unsupported.","title":"Requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"#Setup","page":"Home","text":"No separate BART installation or path configuration is needed:\n\n    using BartIO\n    bart(0, \"version\")\n\nset_bart_path(\"/path/to/bart\") overrides the executable; set_bart_path(\"\") restores the bundled one.","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-to-use-BartIO.jl","page":"Home","text":"BART functions ca be called, e.g., by either of the to calls:\n\nbart(0,\"version\")\nk_phant = bart(1,\"phantom -x64 -k\")\n\nIn the first example, the leading argument 0 indicates that bart will not return anything. In the second example, the leading 1 indicates that bart will return 1 object. For certain functions, you will have provide BART with data, which can be done in the following way:\n\ntraj = bart(1,\"traj -x 128 -y 256 -r\")\nk_phant = bart(1,\"phantom -k -t\", traj)\nim_phant = bart(1,\"nufft -i\", traj, k_phant)\n\nwhere traj and k_phant are arrays of ComplexF32 (BART works with single precision). Note, if you pass multiple arguments, they are concatenated at the end of the command line. The last 2 lines are equivalent to the command line call\n\nbart phantom -k -t traj k_phant\nbart nufft -i traj k_phant im_phant\n\nAlternatively you can pass optional arrays with keywords\n\nk_phant = bart(1,\"phantom -k\", t=traj)\n\nTo print all available BART functions, you can call bart() without arguments:\n\nbart()\n\nIf you need help for the function you can use:\n\nbart(0,\"pics -h\")","title":"How to use BartIO.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Alternatives","page":"Home","text":"As an alternative, you can, from within Julia, manually perform a system call of BART:\n\npathto_bart_exec=\"/home/CODE/bart-master/bart\"\nrun(`$pathto_bart_exec version`)","title":"Alternatives"}]
}
